Here's a table of your response counts:

> apply(dat, 2, table)
      V1   V2   V3   V4   V5   V6   V7   V8   V9
0 4726    3 9677 9948 9996 8116 9724 5830  193
1 5274 9997  323   52    4 1884  276 4170 9807

Notice that some items have 4/10000 positive endorsements or 3/10000 negative endorsements, which is why a partitioned total score method like S_X2 fails when collapsing the response counts. E.g., compare the default which collapses cells with expected values less than 1:

> itemfit(fit)
  item    S_X2 df.S_X2 RMSEA.S_X2 p.S_X2
1   V1 339.126       2      0.130  0.000
2   V2      NA      NA         NA     NA
3   V3 231.441       3      0.087  0.000
4   V4  28.538       2      0.036  0.000
5   V5     NaN       0        NaN    NaN
6   V6 111.425       2      0.074  0.000
7   V7 128.721       3      0.065  0.000
8   V8 247.343       2      0.111  0.000
9   V9   3.754       1      0.017  0.053

to no collapsing at all (with terrible theoretical sampling performance):

> itemfit(fit, mincell = 0)
  item    S_X2 df.S_X2 RMSEA.S_X2 p.S_X2
1   V1 400.200       6      0.081  0.000
2   V2   1.181       6      0.000  0.978
3   V3 232.733       6      0.061  0.000
4   V4  34.214       6      0.022  0.000
5   V5  37.210       6      0.023  0.000
6   V6 146.724       6      0.048  0.000
7   V7 129.385       6      0.045  0.000
8   V8 274.693       6      0.067  0.000
9   V9   5.636       6      0.000  0.465

Hence, due to the overall sparseness of the counts the conditional total-score method breaks and become unreliable.

Phil

